WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.440
- Making responsible

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.090
and effective COVID-19 viz is hard.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.620
And so really quickly going to
go through a couple of places

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.620
where people kinda fell short

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.030
when trying to visualize COVID-19.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.430
Some common pitfalls with
visualizing this data,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.123
this graph was shown in a
local Fox News broadcast.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.210
Aesthetically, it's definitely
not the prettiest graph,

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.103
but if you look carefully at the y-axis,

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.980
you'll notice something weird.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.770
So the number is jumping
in weird intervals, right?

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.270
So this is not something we
really want in a y-axis graph.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.713
And for those who know about log graphs,

00:00:54.713 --> 00:00:58.430
this is not that, because you'll see here,

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:02.897
we're jumping by 30, 30, 10, 30.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.940
Me trying to do math quickly is difficult.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:06.773
30, 30,

00:01:11.162 --> 00:01:12.667
50, 10, right?

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:17.860
The intervals are strange.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.860
This is about 50 jump up here,
too, which is very bizarre.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.800
I personally have actually not seen

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.670
a y-axis that weird and in a while

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.280
and it's kinda rare to see it that weird,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.070
but think about the audience
in this case, right?

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:37.470
Someone watching TV,
their local news station,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.590
is just looking at this graph quickly,

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.560
is taking in like, all
right, cases up here,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.193
time is happening this way,

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.100
and they're looking at new cases per day.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.480
And this kind of was shown in April,

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.510
so this was a while back
now, so don't, yeah.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.840
This is a very low number

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.460
compared to where we're at right now.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.470
But these inconsistent intervals

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.180
actually make these case
jumps on a regular basis

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.590
seem either smaller or
bigger than they should be.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.580
So if someone is casually
just watching this on TV,

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.060
people can make their own inferences

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.684
and conclusions about that, right?

00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:16.210
They're going to be like,
"Oh, this huge jump here,"

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.500
which actually, you know,
might not actually be that big

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.953
because of how the y-axis is being drawn,

00:02:24.116 --> 00:02:26.350
might be like, "Oh, what
was happening at that time?

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.040
Well, then this must
have caused it," right?

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.100
Like there's so much
space for a casual viewer

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.680
to jump and make inferences

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.580
about what's happening with this data.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.450
The other downside to COVID viz

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.450
is that if it's done kind
of lazily or not well,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.300
it can be fodder for conspiracies

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.270
around the spread of COVID.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.880
Many of these types of conspiracies

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.430
are supported by maps like these.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.020
So what we have here

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.490
is actually two completely separate maps,

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.763
just sort of juxtaposed
on top of each other.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:07.020
The top one is claiming
that countries, you know,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.120
with 5G, labeled here in
this sort of red color,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:18.500
seem to be overlapping with
countries that have COVID cases.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.870
So if you look, actually,
there's a lot here, right?

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.363
But if you look really
carefully at this bottom map,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.020
you'll notice that the
shading isn't, you know,

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:32.060
presence versus absence, it's
not just like one shade of red

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.800
and then sort of like beigey up here.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.250
There are many shades of red,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.700
but there's no scale to
indicate what that means.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.960
All that's written here is
that red cases, or sorry,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.780
red countries with hundred
or more confirmed cases,

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.603
and beige countries with
fewer than a hundred cases.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.993
Granted, this was earlier
on in the pandemic.

00:03:55.687 --> 00:04:00.687
A hundred cases in a country
is actually an important number

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:04.880
but it actually is more
what epidemiologists

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.300
use to mark the start of a pandemic,

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.940
as opposed to a comparison point.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:14.050
A lot of data viz these days,

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.210
when looking at the
trajectory of the pandemic,

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:19.710
is trying to actually start mapping data

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.400
starting at a hundred days
or, sorry, the day at which

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.200
a country reaches their hundredth case,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.120
because then you can reliably say

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.920
there's an outbreak in that country.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.930
So that, already, is a weird dichotomy

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:36.253
to set up for this map.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.540
The other thing is that countries
are kind of being labeled

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.180
just based on that one metric

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.740
without any kind of
consideration for population.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:52.610
So think about it, right?

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.200
So if a country has a hundred cases,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.240
but they have 300 million people,

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.110
that's really different than
a hundred cases showing up

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.910
in a country with 10 million people.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.680
So right, already we have
a lot of issues here.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.410
But fundamentally the
biggest problem here,

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.620
which some of you might've
already picked up on,

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.660
is this association.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.410
So the person who made
this meme or whatever

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:15.400
put these two together

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.810
and is strongly implying
that these two things

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:20.160
are associated with each
other, are correlated.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.600
Of course, we know that correlation

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.740
does not equal causation.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.480
This cartoon is essentially indicating

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:31.810
this really common phenomenon

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.600
with issues with making maps with data,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.460
especially in the United States.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.240
As an example here, the maps
in the cartoon on the right

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.020
are arguably all much more similar

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.770
to each other than these two are,

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:49.670
but that's because they're all

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:52.623
actually showing the same
thing, which is population.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.640
When you don't normalize for population,

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.210
you can look at lots of different metrics

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.960
that essentially just follow

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.793
where people tend to
cluster across a country.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.980
So population normalization

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.250
is actually one of the
most important things

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.413
you can think about when making viz,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.880
especially when you're trying to compare

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.783
the spread of COVID
across different regions.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.380
That a photo of Trump I showed earlier

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.400
holding up that case
fatality rate bar chart

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.810
with the four countries,

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.370
so that segment was part of, I guess,

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:34.370
now infamous show of Chris
Wallace's Fox News program.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.950
And so right after,
actually, that was aired,

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:44.660
Chris Wallace's show inserted
this sort of fact-check image

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.710
to highlight that, you know,
more than just four countries,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.450
there are more countries on
this list, surprise, surprise,

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.290
there are other countries in the world,

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:56.513
so it now including Italy,
Spain, Brazil, and South Korea.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.970
U.S. does not have

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:04.210
the lowest case fatality
rate in the world.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.030
Again, this is not
comprehensive list of countries,

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:10.053
but it's at least a few more
than what the president showed.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:16.720
And the issue here, right, so
a viewer seeing this is like,

00:07:16.817 --> 00:07:19.880
"Oh, so Trump just like, didn't
include a few more countries

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:20.890
but the United States

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:23.960
still has a pretty low case
fatality rate, so not so bad."

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.970
But actually if you zoom in

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.080
on the top of this
little graph they showed,

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:33.320
so this is from Oxford
researchers who did this graphic.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:34.800
They actually, you know,

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:38.080
the ones who calculated
this case fatality rate,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.230
they weren't against assigning

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.410
too much importance to the metric.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.830
This is kind of not super clear,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:44.820
just 'cause of the quality of the picture,

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.810
but it literally reads, during
an outbreak of a pandemic,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.690
the CFR, the case fatality
rate is a poor measure

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.510
of the mortality risk of the disease.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.410
So, you know, the fact that
people are even using this

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.970
as a metric for assessing how
dangerous this diseases is

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:03.570
is somewhat flawed.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:05.770
But again, right, like
that's just a nuance

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:08.593
that the viewer is just not party to.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.760
They miss that entirely

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.240
because this is just a series of lines

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.190
and they're looking for the lowest one.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.900
So we looked at a couple of examples

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.900
of kind of viz having some
issues with COVID spread,

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.160
and there are plenty more
examples out there of this.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.923
Oftentimes, you know, some of
the mistakes are inadvertent.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:36.750
Most of the time actually,

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.283
I would say they're
inadvertent and not malicious,

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:44.320
but again, this is the job of the person

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.140
creating the visualization
to do this responsibly.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.140
Oftentimes for something like COVID,

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.710
it's not just on one person.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.230
We have, typically teams
of people working on this.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:09:00.570
You have epidemiologists, policy makers,

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.260
like all interfacing with designers

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:06.300
to actually collect the
expertise into one place

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.700
and create the best possible

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:09.710
and most informed visualization.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.350
And that was definitely the case

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.770
for the Financial Times
data journalism team.

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:21.770
So these graphs became really
really popular line graphs,

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.060
became very very popular

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.790
since they kind of started
popping out in March.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.400
So John Burn-Murdoch, who
works at the Financial Times,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:34.400
on March 11th, tweeted this
line graph, which initially,

00:09:35.276 --> 00:09:38.350
I think they're now like
50 iterations of this now.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:39.370
It's many, many versions,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.280
but initially this was
showing the cumulative number

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.373
of COVID cases in major countries.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.153
The Federal Times has a European tilt,

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.300
so some more European
countries are listed here.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:57.130
His graphs were unique in that
he used a logarithmic scale,

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.310
which is actually quite good

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:03.310
because mainly viruses spread
exponentially over time,

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.170
and so this allows for a more clear

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:07.200
direct comparison across countries.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:08.940
You can imagine if this
weren't logarithmic,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.020
you'd have this kind of weird big slope

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.020
for every one of these countries,

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:15.180
that's sort of redundant

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.050
for that initial sort
of exponential phase.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:22.010
And so comparing countries more directly

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:25.750
and using the kind of
real estate of the graph

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:30.750
is kind of enhanced by
using the logarithmic scale.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.430
There are also a lot of
really good design choices.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:37.420
I'm not gonna spend too much time today

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:42.420
talking about, you know, color
theory or accessible design,

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.500
but these are actually really
important things to consider

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.170
when you're getting into data viz.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:50.350
I'll talk a little bit about
that in the coding video,

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:54.730
but a couple of things here that are great

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:58.330
is sort of the color scheme
that John is using here

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:03.150
is really distinctive and
kind of very appropriate

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:05.135
for what he's trying to show, right?

00:11:05.135 --> 00:11:08.070
The lines themselves are colored in a way

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.823
to draw attention to the data.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:14.223
The fonts are all clear and clean.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:16.790
The graph itself is extremely minimal,

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:18.650
so there's really like no clutter,

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:22.360
like you have the absolute minimal

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.660
kind of information you need.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:25.947
So you're not kind of like
spending a lot of time

00:11:25.947 --> 00:11:29.020
trying to visually try to figure out,

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.410
not a lot of cognitive work to figure out

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.870
like what really is happening, right?

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:35.990
And the really, really great
thing that John did with this,

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:38.740
that a lot of great, you know,
data viz practitioners do

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.640
is to use annotations.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:45.640
So here you can see he's written
in a couple of notes here.

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:48.733
So for South Korea, Japan,
Singapore, Hong Kong,

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:54.330
he has this sort of
dotted line to indicate

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.690
the 33% daily increase
like, this is what that is.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.280
And so, you know, if a
line is sort of wavering

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:03.623
above the 33% line,

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.340
you know they're sort of in
this one category of spread

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:12.220
and if they're below, you know
that's more where a country

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:13.830
would want to be.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.917
And so without these
annotations, someone would ask,

00:12:16.917 --> 00:12:19.410
"Okay, so what makes South Korea, Japan,

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:21.060
Singapore, Hong Kong so special?"

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.560
Without the annotations, the
audience has to do that work

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.970
or imagination or whatever it is, right?

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:31.290
But John is doing that
work here for the viewer

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:35.910
by putting in specific policy measures

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.090
that different the countries have taken

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:39.910
as well as some caveats
as well, too, right?

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:44.910
So for example, South Korea
spread slowed from initial pace,

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:46.580
but it's picked up again,

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:50.033
so it's not like all happy-go-lucky there,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:52.440
and Japan's rate is low,

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:55.730
but there's debate about whether
enough tests have been done

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:59.070
to kind of capture the real spread.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:01.673
Singapore, Hong Kong,
super super low spread,

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:04.740
both having strict quarantining

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.693
and very rigorous contact tracing.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.973
Hong Kong also having
rapid school closures.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.470
So this is just a very
simple kind of example

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:16.630
of how you can just add a
little bit extra to a graph

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:20.160
to really make it compelling
and provide that context.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.720
The kind of like point
of this graph, right,

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:24.330
was John has talked to us quite a bit

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:25.430
about his series of graphs,

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:29.300
is to drive the
inevitability of Coronavirus

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.770
and how countries are sort
of going down this same path

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.420
as Italy was at the time,

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:37.300
which was considered sort of ground zero

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:38.993
for Europe for COVID,

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.020
when they don't adopt certain
public health measures

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.820
like quarantines and robust testing.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.823
And Financial Times team plus John,

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.320
they went on to make graphs like these

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.000
for a bunch of other metrics
that became, you know,

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.270
rapidly, as teams kind
of work together, right,

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.660
this is part of like what working

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:00.920
in a collaborative group does.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:02.167
You had epidemiologists being like,

00:14:02.167 --> 00:14:03.110
"Well actually this measure

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.770
is really good to look at as well, too."

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:09.970
So things like excess deaths,
hospitalization, ICU capacity,

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:11.778
these are all very unique
and different metrics

00:14:11.778 --> 00:14:16.260
with which to look at
the spread of COVID-19.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:17.436
On the flip side, right?

00:14:17.436 --> 00:14:20.850
The problem with putting
out a viz to a public space.

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:25.100
So John tweeted this out,
the graph I showed before,

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:26.440
is that there's always room for some sort

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:28.770
of like appropriation
or recontextualization.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:32.330
So this was a few weeks later.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:33.870
John had another graphs, again,

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.870
it's another cumulative number of cases.

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:39.290
Number of days since
hundredth case, remember that

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:41.940
I mentioned as being kind
of like the gold standard

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:45.310
for, this is when the
country has an outbreak,

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:47.310
so we're gonna start counting from here.

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:51.967
Someone saw John's graph and was like,

00:14:51.967 --> 00:14:56.830
"All right, I'm gonna
circle all of the countries

00:14:56.830 --> 00:15:01.830
with this lower trajectories,
lower spread of cases

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:07.770
and the ones above and just
give them their own sort of,

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.230
you know, annotation."

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:12.040
In this case, it's no masks versus masks.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.467
This is obviously a really
aggressive simplification

00:15:14.467 --> 00:15:16.797
of the differences between
two sets of countries

00:15:16.797 --> 00:15:21.797
and it's kind of masked by the
big red and big blue circles,

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:26.610
but there are a lot of countries
in this no mask category.

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.970
There's the U.S., there's Turkey,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.703
there's the UK, Brazil, Spain.

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:36.513
I guess Italy is kind
of in there as well too.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:41.670
And so I think it's not too difficult

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:45.300
to see how those countries
cannot all be sort of summarized

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.113
into mask, non-mask adhering countries.

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:52.300
If we know anything, we know mask wearing

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.610
is something that is extremely variable

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:56.660
across populations within a country,

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.610
let alone across countries,

00:15:58.610 --> 00:15:59.900
but there are other things happening

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:00.733
in each of these countries.

00:16:00.733 --> 00:16:04.930
Like I mentioned, mitigating
factors like policy changes,

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.380
demographic differences,
things like that, right?

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.440
Whether or not quarantines
were being enforced,

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:12.950
test and trace programs,

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:14.530
all of this stuff is happening

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.190
at the same time as data collection.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:18.687
And so for someone to
go in and just be like,

00:16:18.687 --> 00:16:21.250
"Well, solved this problem.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.610
It's no mask versus mask,"

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.430
is a gross simplification.

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.840
The sad thing is, underneath
all this kind of like garish,

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:31.150
like annotation, John
actually did a lot of work

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:33.390
to annotate the different countries

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.180
to kind of provide context
for what's happening

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:36.330
in different countries.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.140
But, you know, when you're
sharing something on social media

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:44.840
this is something to be really wary of

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:47.480
because people want to
apply their own narratives

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.860
to data that they see.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:53.200
This is something that's
happened time and time again.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:54.930
People come in to looking at data

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:58.120
with their own preconceived biases,

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:01.390
and they love data that kinda confirms it.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.620
Even if it doesn't actually confirm it,

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.770
they can kind of warp and annotate things

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:08.993
to fit their own narratives.

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:15.010
Another phenomenal example of
COVID-19 viz this past year

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:20.070
has been from the Reuters graphic team.

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:22.520
So again, strongly,
strongly, strongly recommend

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:25.470
all of their work, it's
absolutely gorgeous.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:28.700
They have a live Coronavirus case tracker

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:32.420
in the United States, which is
one of my personal favorites.

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:34.910
So this is updated regularly.

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:39.910
I'm looking at it right now,
updated today, January 4th.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:45.610
And yeah, I'm just gonna
scroll through this

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:49.410
just to highlight a few things here.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:54.410
So yeah, I first wanna say
like a very common thing

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:59.470
with data viz that you'll
see online that's wonderful,

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:00.810
I'm gonna show a couple examples of this,

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.510
is what's called scrollytelly,

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:05.470
which is kind of a silly phrase,

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:08.610
but the idea is, much the way you read

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:11.530
like any kind of article online, you know,

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.090
you're scrolling through a page.

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:15.060
But instead of just text,

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:19.520
you're getting visuals to
provide context and distill data.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:21.890
So in this case, this is
sort of what Reuters does.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.270
The thing I wanna focus
on their tracker page,

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:25.870
there's a lot more on this page,

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:31.100
is their sort of mini
map across the states.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.460
So here, right, you probably recognize

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:35.840
this is a representation of
the United States of America.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:38.133
Each state, instead of being a state,

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:42.000
is represented by a trend line.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:45.100
Again, a line graph, so
time being on the x-axis,

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:50.100
and the y-axis is the
weekly reported cases

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:53.530
per 100,000 in the population.

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:58.010
So right away, we see that
this is a normalized number.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:18:59.040
And why is it normalized?

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.823
Because that was what makes
it comparable across states.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:05.017
There are states with a lot of people

00:19:05.017 --> 00:19:07.300
and there are states with
not that many people.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:09.530
So if you don't do this
type of normalization,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:12.080
your comparisons are gonna
be completely messed up.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.780
The other cool thing here for the users,

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:18.810
that you can actually flip
between an adjusted scale

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:20.133
and a uniform scale.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:22.997
And so what this means, you might be like,

00:19:22.997 --> 00:19:26.410
"Oh, that's a cool
feature, you can toggle."

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:28.210
So Reuters actually tells you.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:29.450
It's pretty small down here,

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:31.350
but your uniform scale shows

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:33.870
all the states on the
same vertical axis, right?

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:38.870
So imagining these are like
pure case numbers, in a way,

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.010
so that they're comparable, right?

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:47.110
New York having this many
cases and this many cases,

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.840
and things like that,
versus the adjusted scale,

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.460
which emphasizes the
trajectory in each state

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:55.600
by recalibrating the vertical axis

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:57.400
according to the state's
highest weekly figure.

00:19:57.400 --> 00:19:59.300
So this is more of like,

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:03.623
this is the shape of the pandemic
trajectory in this state.

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:06.350
And this is a more kind of like,

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:08.160
if we're gonna set the
scales to be uniform

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:10.903
across the different states.

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:14.933
And the other feature here,

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:20.440
so this is a little like
kind of yellow shading

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:25.150
where cases have risen
for more than two weeks.

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:27.130
So in this case, New
York, Virginia, Georgia

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:28.283
are highlighted here.

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:30.530
This obviously changes over time.

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:31.440
Over the course of the pandemic

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:33.360
this has looked very different.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:35.220
But these are helpful metrics, right,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:40.220
to kind of compare severity
and kind of aggressiveness

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:42.383
of the spread of the pandemic.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.340
The other feature here,

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:47.310
a lot of the stuff I was showing before

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:49.520
was cumulative cases,

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:52.200
so like literally raw numbers of cases.

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.430
But what this is, is
weekly reported cases.

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:57.910
So this is at the end of every week

00:20:57.910 --> 00:20:59.263
as of Sunday each week.

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:05.840
These are the sort of
cases for that batch.

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.530
The reason why this is done weekly

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:12.660
is to kind of distill
the potential effects

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:15.720
of any single day being
a particular problem.

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:19.913
So one very common issue with COVID data,

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:23.270
departments of health
across different states,

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:26.448
and countries of course,
collect data very differently

00:21:26.448 --> 00:21:30.480
and individual days might
have issues with reporting.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:34.840
So a country can simply just, you know,

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:35.700
a country, excuse me,

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:40.700
a state can have like a
data entry issue one day.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:44.010
Maybe, you know, we just had the holidays,

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:48.200
like there could have
been a lag in reporting.

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:49.560
There could've been like a system failure

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:53.110
with like where the data was stored.

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:54.910
Like there are a whole bunch
of things that could happen

00:21:54.910 --> 00:21:58.410
with data entry or there
could be like an actual issue

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:02.550
with the way in which data is collected.

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.870
So maybe data was
initially classed as tests

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:08.573
and said those were cases
and stuff like this happens.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.680
And so you wanna kinda remove the noise

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:13.547
of individual days and so this

00:22:13.547 --> 00:22:18.547
is why it's weekly reported
cases as opposed to daily cases.

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:25.570
And another phenomenal
data viz from this year

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:28.380
was from Harry Stevens
at The Washington Post.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:30.470
Again, their graphics team is phenomenal,

00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:32.220
so definitely recommend their work.

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:37.870
This was a very, very cool and
unique type of visualization.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.520
So this was at the very
start of the pandemic,

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:41.883
since this was March 14th.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:49.530
This actually became the
number one most visited site

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:53.260
in The Washington Post's
site history ever.

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:55.547
It's translated into many languages now,

00:22:55.547 --> 00:22:56.773
so you can see here.

00:22:58.593 --> 00:22:59.670
And so really briefly,

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.470
I'm gonna jump through this page.

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:06.000
Again, it's one of these
like scroll-type situations.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.180
And what this was trying to do

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:13.450
is to essentially use simulations

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.110
to explain how the virus spreads over time

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:19.740
by creating the sort of
fake virus called simulitis,

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.540
kind of acting in lieu of COVID.

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:25.780
But just to understand, you know,

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:28.160
why actually social distancing measures

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:32.040
and measures like those actually work

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:34.003
to flatten the curve per se, right?

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:36.520
So right, this starts off, again,

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:37.850
this is a while ago now.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:39.613
It feels like years ago,

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:43.070
but this was sort of like how cases

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.413
initially kicked off in the U.S.

00:23:47.137 --> 00:23:48.580
It does a really good job of breaking down

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:52.000
some of the jargon around
COVID, initially, too,

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.960
which I'm sure would be helpful

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:56.990
to kind of any person reading it.

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:57.910
But the idea here, right,

00:23:57.910 --> 00:23:59.700
they create a fake disease, simulitis.

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:02.300
The idea is that it spreads pretty easily,

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:03.540
so whenever a healthy person

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:05.210
comes into contact with a sick person,

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:07.060
that healthy person becomes sick too.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:10.760
Just like this, kind of
just like when one of these

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:12.790
little like balls of virus,

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:14.640
balls of people, I should say, touch.

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:19.423
It's pretty quick for everyone
to catch this fake disease.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:23.100
But then, of course,
people recover over time.

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:26.830
So again, this is a situation

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:27.860
where we're making assumptions.

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:29.003
This is not COVID.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:32.760
People are also actually
dying of the disease too,

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:33.880
but this is assuming that people

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:36.800
are recovering from the disease.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:39.540
And so just to highlight
the spread of this, right,

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.590
essentially the way
this visualization works

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:44.150
is Harry is having simulations run.

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:46.220
So it's a new simulation
every time you run it

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:50.690
of what happens to the
kind of recovered, healthy,

00:24:50.690 --> 00:24:53.883
and sick population under
different scenarios.

00:24:56.610 --> 00:25:01.610
So in this case, yeah, we have,
it's a little bit glitchy,

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:07.363
so sorry about that.

00:25:09.751 --> 00:25:11.220
A lot of people are
getting sick pretty fast.

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:12.663
This curve is big.

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:16.720
Over time, people do
recover, but you know,

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:19.570
a lot of people had to get
sick first for this to happen.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:23.763
And this is a relatively small population.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:29.130
There are 330 million
people in the United States,

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.050
so that curve would
steepen for a long time

00:25:32.050 --> 00:25:33.350
before it started to slow.

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:40.023
Now, in a case that China
tried in the Hubei province,

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:43.290
they tried a forced quarantine.

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:47.350
So this is sort of what
that simulation is like,

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:50.820
where we quarantine off
the sick population.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:55.820
But of course, forced quarantines
actually don't really work

00:25:55.840 --> 00:26:00.840
because, as Harry mentions
here, it's not realistic,

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:07.080
so people can't actually
be quarantined entirely

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:09.320
and there are always going to be leaks.

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:11.210
And so those leaks eventually

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:15.340
impact the rest of the population.

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:18.440
What happens when 3/4 of a population

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:21.240
adopt social distancing?

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.307
So you see some of the
balls sort of stop moving

00:26:24.307 --> 00:26:28.180
while some of the other little
balls continue moving around,

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:31.513
and let's see what happens
with this trajectory.

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:35.080
We're literally seeing the
flattening of the curve

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:37.750
happen in real time in the simulation.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.070
So, you know, right
around this time in March,

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:42.170
there was a lot happening

00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:44.600
with that flatten the
curve kind of mantra.

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:45.467
But a lot of people were like,

00:26:45.467 --> 00:26:48.730
"Well, why, I don't really get
why I'm doing this entirely.

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:51.220
Like I sort of get like I'm supposed to,

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:52.610
but it doesn't make sense."

00:26:52.610 --> 00:26:54.380
And so this did a really really good job

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:58.050
of explaining this kind
of public health stance

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:00.243
that was taken early on in the pandemic.

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:05.280
Where even fewer people are moving around,

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:06.919
so just one out of every eight people

00:27:06.919 --> 00:27:09.870
are moving around while other people

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.433
are sort of social
distancing in this case.

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:19.550
And that curve is about as
flat as it can be in this case.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:21.570
Again, this is a fake disease.

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:24.790
There are so many other
kind of aspects to COVID

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:26.550
that are different than this.

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:29.630
But in terms of conveying
of pretty complex

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:33.050
epidemiological and public health concept,

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:36.560
this does really really
good job of distilling down

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:40.070
why things like social distancing work

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:41.320
even if at a moderate level.

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:45.070
So, right, as a summary of this, you know,

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:48.323
how flattening the curve
can look like in real time.

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:53.780
This is sort of what we experience

00:27:53.780 --> 00:27:56.180
and these simulations are, again,

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:59.540
this is generated every
time someone loads the page.

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:00.940
And so there's this really kind of like

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:03.140
strong interactive feeling to this

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:05.543
and exploring different cases,

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:08.900
different conditions to see like outcomes

00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:12.100
and how data changes
according to those outcomes.

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:16.480
And probably my favorite
set of visualizations

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:20.680
from the New York Times
graphics department.

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:23.680
They produce some of the
absolute best data viz

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.230
that I've ever kind of seen.

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:27.240
And their COVID work was remarkable,

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:28.810
some of their best work.

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:30.987
They had a sort of pair of articles,

00:28:30.987 --> 00:28:32.040
"How the Virus Got Out,"

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.550
which was sort of from the China end,

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:37.610
exploring the viruses trajectory
out to other countries.

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:39.370
But the "How the Virus Won" story

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:40.730
is the one I wanna focus on

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:44.123
because this is focused
on the United States.

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:47.170
So I'm gonna just
quickly skim through this

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:50.603
and highlight a few things
that this does extremely well.

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:55.350
So again, this is just sort
of the scrollytelly format

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:57.670
where you're scrolling through

00:28:57.670 --> 00:29:00.410
and you're kind of getting new information

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:02.420
broken down into kind of
like bite-sized chunks.

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:06.490
And so this makes it very,
you know, easy to digest

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:08.237
for someone just kind of
coming in and being like,

00:29:08.237 --> 00:29:10.630
"I don't know how to ingest this data."

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:13.050
So the flip side to the static graphs,

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:14.920
which are also great, 'cause
they're just, you know,

00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:15.900
very kind of efficient.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:16.990
You just kind of like see it

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:19.170
and you'd get your takeaway.

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:21.710
Interactives take take a
bit more time to go through,

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:24.430
but they can kind of, in that respect,

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:25.703
cover a lot more ground.

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:32.160
And so starting off from the
first cases in the States,

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:35.430
known cases, I should say,

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:38.180
and sort of following
the story of those cases.

00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:40.230
But then actually tracking this on a map,

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:44.450
talking about hidden infections,

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:46.630
which we now know was a huge problem.

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:47.910
We didn't have adequate testing,

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:51.530
so we did not know and have
any sense of the spread

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:53.163
of this disease at the outset.

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:58.370
Over 2,000 infections spreading
throughout major cities.

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:02.350
And we tried some travel restrictions,

00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:04.290
but they weren't full bans.

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:06.050
Lots of infected travelers coming in

00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:07.260
from the rest of the world, right?

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:10.290
'Cause this has already
been spreading for a bit

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:13.370
and seeded initial
outbreaks in the country.

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:14.940
And then over this kind of critical window

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:16.450
that now we were very familiar with

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:18.980
where we didn't have the kind of testing

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:21.790
to understand the extent
of these outbreaks.

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:25.120
Meanwhile, the virus is
just spreading very happily.

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:26.952
And again, this is also being paired

00:30:26.952 --> 00:30:28.930
with what else is
happening in the country.

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:33.930
Policy decisions, changes in travel

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:36.610
that don't really kick in
really until March, right?

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:41.300
And so from March 1st to March 14th,

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:42.810
a lot of people are still moving

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:44.250
very normally across the country.

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:45.840
This is cell phone data.

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:48.017
So now we're getting into
this really cool thing

00:30:48.017 --> 00:30:48.850
the New York Times does,

00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:50.510
is overlaying different types of data.

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:54.890
And throughout this, right,
they're also making, you know,

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:57.217
they're pausing with each
new dataset and saying,

00:30:57.217 --> 00:30:59.190
"Hey, this is this data we have,

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.400
this is how it's aggregated,
this is the data source,"

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:02.974
which is really important to do

00:31:02.974 --> 00:31:05.493
for someone just kind
of like coming into this

00:31:05.493 --> 00:31:08.273
and not really knowing
what to get out of this.

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:12.410
Again, you have date
stamps for everything.

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:14.210
We're zooming in on specific locations

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:15.840
to get a better picture.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:17.690
The text blurbs are very helpful

00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:20.630
to kind of frame the data story.

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:21.580
And also throughout this,

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:24.940
I'll say like the kind
of color choices here

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:26.154
are very powerful, right?

00:31:26.154 --> 00:31:30.150
Your eyes immediately
go to these very stark

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:32.320
kind of the blue, the red,

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:35.640
they're not these kind of like
bold sort of blue red colors.

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:36.540
They're muted in a way,

00:31:36.540 --> 00:31:38.630
but everything else is sort of minimalized

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:39.870
to be absolutely essential,

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:41.390
again, to declutter the picture

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:43.973
and to focus on the critical parts.

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:47.530
Love the great quotes in here, too,

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:51.320
from politicians who made choices.

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:52.500
This is one of the, in my opinion,

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:55.040
one of the most powerful pieces of viz

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:55.893
that the New York Times did

00:31:55.893 --> 00:31:59.150
because it does this very
kind of like whole picture

00:31:59.150 --> 00:32:03.360
look at what was happening at
every stage in this process.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:05.570
And then even beyond
travel, movement data,

00:32:05.570 --> 00:32:09.490
now we're getting into the
actual genetic mutation data,

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:11.690
a whole 'nother type of data, but right,

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:13.110
they're able to take this, as well, too,

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:14.760
to study the spread

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:16.963
and talk about different
variants of COVID.

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.930
Again, visualizing this in
an extremely compelling way.

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:25.460
And by March 13th, when the
lockdowns kind of started,

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:29.050
and that was when travel
was limited from Europe.

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:30.760
The outbreak was already
kind of, you know,

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:32.880
in full force across the country.

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:34.570
There's obviously a huge amount of data

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:36.100
that goes into what they're showing

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:38.790
in terms of hotspot
data across the country,

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:40.770
where cases are breaking out,

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:42.450
but the story also stops

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:47.170
and takes a minute to look
at very specific cases.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:50.410
So in this case, centering
on this one woman

00:32:50.410 --> 00:32:52.880
who traveled from New Orleans

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:55.940
where we now know there was
an outbreak at that time

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:59.980
to Memphis, helping seed a case there.

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:01.210
So again, this is something similar

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:03.720
to what the Washington Post piece did,

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:07.640
in that they distill things

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:10.040
down to the individual level, right?

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:11.780
One ball kind of infecting another ball.

00:33:11.780 --> 00:33:14.450
In this case, it's how
one person's movement

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:19.260
went and then seeded
an entire new outbreak.

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:20.960
And in many ways, this is kind of similar

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:25.960
to that kind of like abstract
balls floating around,

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:28.601
but the context here with the text,

00:33:28.601 --> 00:33:29.900
this is real data.

00:33:29.900 --> 00:33:31.593
This is not a fake virus, right?

00:33:32.870 --> 00:33:35.810
Of who was traveling where,

00:33:35.810 --> 00:33:38.120
what was it's happening in
the country at the time.

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:40.570
And this is when the
lockdown sort of kicked off.

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:45.690
This article came out in June,

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:49.150
and so this was tracking
sort of movement over time.

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:50.420
I'm also going through this very quickly,

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:52.090
so please take your time with this.

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:52.923
I just wanna kind of highlight

00:33:52.923 --> 00:33:56.620
some of the the cool things
I liked about this piece,

00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:58.230
that are really powerful.

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:03.230
Again, the color is really very minimal,

00:34:03.650 --> 00:34:05.800
but it's powerful
because it's highlighting

00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:13.110
the most specific and important
aspects of this story.

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:15.030
So we kinda slowed exponential growth

00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:16.970
from those early reductions of movements,

00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:19.269
and just thinking back
to those simulations

00:34:19.269 --> 00:34:21.530
of flattening the curve.

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:22.363
Being in New York City, right,

00:34:22.363 --> 00:34:24.960
this is specific to
what was happening here.

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:26.040
Again, going back to the idea

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:29.640
that different regions
are very hard to compare

00:34:29.640 --> 00:34:32.263
unless you control for
many different factors.

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:37.460
So the kind of politics and
logistics and infrastructure

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:40.120
of New York plays a very
different role there

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:43.490
in how daily life is affected.

00:34:43.490 --> 00:34:46.190
It's not just the disease at play.

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:49.670
Kind of continuing now to the deaths data.

00:34:49.670 --> 00:34:53.110
So before it was cases,
and now move to deaths,

00:34:53.110 --> 00:34:55.410
which of course were
extremely, extremely bad,

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:59.110
April, May, in New York City.

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:04.110
And this piece of data
viz also has a point.

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:11.480
At this juncture, there
was the main kind of story

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:13.790
of not enough testing
happening in the country,

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:16.770
and so this story has this message

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:20.040
of we don't actually
know how bad it can be

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:22.660
because we still can't, you know,

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:26.010
test the people that we need to test.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:28.230
And this has been a problem to this day,

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:30.710
but at the time that was
extremely problematic.

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:33.930
Testing hadn't really ramped
up to the level it needed to.

00:35:33.930 --> 00:35:37.150
And, you know, by late June,

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:41.160
we had outbreaks seeded
across the west and south.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:43.920
And this might be the part
people tend to gloss over,

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:47.680
but this is actually really
really important for data viz.

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:50.610
This is a really intense
rundown, and detailed rundown,

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:53.320
of exactly how they did
everything they did here, right?

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:54.980
How they estimated different sets of data,

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:57.940
how they collected different sets of data,

00:35:57.940 --> 00:36:00.040
the limitations of different sets of data.

00:36:00.911 --> 00:36:02.130
I'm not gonna go through
every single one of these,

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:05.290
but right, who they
collaborated with, right?

00:36:05.290 --> 00:36:06.700
There are so many different people

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:07.770
they're interfacing with here.

00:36:07.770 --> 00:36:10.040
So there are scientists they're talking to

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:11.453
for the genetic samples,

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:15.110
companies that are collecting
different types of data,

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:19.349
the cell phone data that was collected

00:36:19.349 --> 00:36:22.470
to kinda capture movement.

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:25.730
But again, there are limitations
to all these things, too.

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:30.280
How they classify different
areas and then conclusions

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.763
and quotes from even
politicians as well, too.

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:35.153
And on top of that,

00:36:35.153 --> 00:36:36.700
more sourcing for their
story as well, too.

00:36:36.700 --> 00:36:38.630
So this is just like
extremely comprehensive way

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:39.880
of telling a story.

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:42.500
This is obviously not
your typical data viz,

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:44.510
but it's a kind of like gold standard

00:36:44.510 --> 00:36:47.310
for how we can break down the story

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:49.240
of the spread of the virus

00:36:49.240 --> 00:36:51.547
in an extremely, extremely thorough way.

